{"parentUuid":"daee8868-e01f-49fd-81cb-5e413dcc4e5b","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/gohan/ga-runners/actions-runner-security-agent-3/_work/security-agent/security-agent","sessionId":"8eebacc1-bc61-4b93-8b89-82f1919148c6","version":"2.1.33","gitBranch":"audit_reth_fusaka-audit_8e65a1d1a2_20260206085440","agentId":"a640cbf","slug":"humming-marinating-flame","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019BX5vpivM7ANk9dRseX6wJ","type":"tool_result","content":"outputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W4B5_1770345716.json:1846:        \"text\": \"coset_fft_field (SG-005) with shift_factor = PRIMITIVE_ROOT_OF_UNITY MUST evaluate polynomials on a coset that does not intersect the FFT evaluation domain. Specifically, for all x in the coset domain and all y in the FFT domain, x != y. This guarantees that division by the vanishing polynomial in recovery (SG-023) never encounters division by zero.\",\noutputs/02_PARTIAL_W5B6_1770346036.json:27:      \"title\": \"Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Does multiply_polynomialcoeff (SG-009) assert polynomial degree bounds before multiplication?\",\noutputs/02_PARTIAL_W5B6_1770346036.json:39:      \"test_procedure\": \"1. Locate multiply_polynomialcoeff implementation. 2. Verify assertion: len(a) + len(b) <= FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_EXT_BLOB. 3. Craft inputs exceeding bounds and verify rejection. 4. Verify product polynomial degree equals deg(a) + deg(b).\",\noutputs/02_PARTIAL_W5B6_1770346036.json:47:      \"title\": \"Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Does interpolate_polynomialcoeff (SG-011) reject duplicate x-values?\",\noutputs/02_PARTIAL_W5B6_1770346036.json:59:      \"test_procedure\": \"1. Locate interpolate_polynomialcoeff implementation. 2. Verify duplicate x-value rejection or crash on division by zero. 3. Submit batch verification with duplicate cell_indices. 4. Verify duplicate x-values cause rejection before reaching interpolation.\",\noutputs/02_PARTIAL_W5B6_1770346036.json:148:      \"title\": \"Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Does coset_shift_for_cell (SG-016) validate cell_index bounds?\",\noutputs/02_PARTIAL_W5B6_1770346036.json:847:      \"test_procedure\": \"1. Verify BRP in polynomial_eval_to_coeff (SG-007). 2. Verify BRP in coset_shift_for_cell (SG-016). 3. Verify BRP in coset_for_cell (SG-017). 4. Verify BRP in recover_polynomialcoeff (SG-023). 5. Cross-reference all paths for consistency.\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1065:        \"text\": \"In compute_cells (SG-018), each cell MUST represent polynomial evaluations over a distinct coset. The output MUST contain exactly CELLS_PER_EXT_BLOB cells, and cell[i] MUST correspond to evaluations over coset_for_cell(i).\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1102:        \"text\": \"In coset_for_cell (SG-017), the coset elements MUST form a coset h*G = {h, h*g, h*g^2, ...} where h = coset_shift_for_cell(cell_index) and g is a generator of the subgroup of order FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_CELL.\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1108:          \"primary_element\": \"SG-017:coset_for_cell\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1137:        \"text\": \"In add_polynomialcoeff (SG-008), the result degree MUST equal max(deg(a), deg(b)). The shorter polynomial MUST be correctly zero-padded for missing coefficients during addition.\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1143:          \"primary_element\": \"SG-008:add_polynomialcoeff\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1172:        \"text\": \"In multiply_polynomialcoeff (SG-009), the result degree MUST equal deg(a) + deg(b). The convolution of coefficients MUST be correctly computed.\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1178:          \"primary_element\": \"SG-009:multiply_polynomialcoeff\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1207:        \"text\": \"In divide_polynomialcoeff (SG-010), the quotient and remainder MUST satisfy a = quotient * b + remainder. The remainder is discarded, which is correct only when the division is exact (remainder is zero).\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1213:          \"primary_element\": \"SG-010:divide_polynomialcoeff\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1242:        \"text\": \"In interpolate_polynomialcoeff (SG-011), the result polynomial MUST have degree at most len(xs) - 1, and MUST satisfy evaluate(result, xs[i]) == ys[i] for all i. This is the Lagrange interpolation correctness property.\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1248:          \"primary_element\": \"SG-011:interpolate_polynomialcoeff\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1277:        \"text\": \"In coset_fft_field (SG-005), the forward coset FFT MUST shift values by shift_factor before applying FFT, and the inverse coset FFT MUST apply inverse FFT then unshift by shift_factor.inverse(). The composition forward(inverse(x)) MUST equal x.\",\noutputs/02_ASYNC_QUEUE_W0B9_1770345716.json:1283:          \"primary_element\": \"SG-005:coset_fft_field\",\noutputs/01e_ASYNC_QUEUE_W4B5_1770296799.json:265:            \"text\": \"Bit-Reversal Permutation Consistency: bit_reversal_permutation is applied consistently in polynomial_eval_to_coeff (SG-007), coset_shift_for_cell (SG-016), coset_for_cell (SG-017), and recover_polynomialcoeff (SG-023). Any inconsistency in permutation application between encoding and decoding paths would produce misaligned evaluations.\"\noutputs/01e_ASYNC_QUEUE_W4B5_1770296799.json:289:            \"text\": \"Non-Zero Divisor in Polynomial Division: divide_polynomialcoeff (SG-010 INV-001) assumes the divisor polynomial b is not the zero polynomial, and that the leading coefficient b[bpos] is nonzero (INV-003). Division by zero would cause a runtime error or undefined behavior.\"\noutputs/01e_ASYNC_QUEUE_W4B5_1770296799.json:293:            \"text\": \"Lagrange Interpolation Point Distinctness: interpolate_polynomialcoeff (SG-011 INV-001) requires all x-values to be distinct for the weight_adjustment inverse (xs[i] - xs[j]).inverse() to exist. Duplicate x-values would cause division by zero in the field, crashing or producing incorrect interpolation.\"\noutputs/01e_ASYNC_QUEUE_W4B5_1770296799.json:483:            \"mitigation\": \"INV-001 of SG-007 constrains the input to exactly FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_BLOB evaluations. INV-002 requires bit-reversal before inverse FFT. The assertion in multiply_polynomialcoeff (SG-009 INV-001: len(a) + len(b) <= FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_EXT_BLOB) provides an explicit degree bound check.\"\noutputs/01e_PARTIAL_W0B1_1770297289.json:1748:          \"text\": \"divide_polynomialcoeff MUST NOT be called with a zero divisor polynomial. The leading coefficient of the divisor MUST be nonzero. Division by zero MUST be prevented by callers.\",\noutputs/01e_ASYNC_QUEUE_W5B14_1770296799.json:236:              \"text\": \"The bit-reversal permutation applied in polynomial_eval_to_coeff, coset_for_cell, and coset_shift_for_cell correctly maps between evaluation-domain ordering and the canonical ordering expected by FFT. An incorrect permutation would produce wrong polynomial coefficients, leading to invalid proofs and cells.\",\noutputs/01e_ASYNC_QUEUE_W5B14_1770296799.json:254:              \"text\": \"The division operations in divide_polynomialcoeff assume a non-zero divisor polynomial (leading coefficient b[bpos] != 0). If the vanishing polynomial is zero or has a zero leading coefficient, division produces undefined results. The vanishing polynomial is constructed from missing cell roots; if all cells are present (no missing roots), the vanishing polynomial is the constant 1, which is valid.\",\noutputs/01e_ASYNC_QUEUE_W5B14_1770296799.json:278:              \"text\": \"Polynomial multiplication in multiply_polynomialcoeff enforces that len(a) + len(b) <= FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_EXT_BLOB. This prevents the product polynomial from exceeding the extended evaluation domain. If this assertion is bypassed, subsequent FFT operations would produce incorrect results or buffer overflows.\",\noutputs/01e_ASYNC_QUEUE_W5B14_1770296799.json:287:              \"text\": \"The Lagrange interpolation in interpolate_polynomialcoeff requires all xs values to be distinct. If duplicate xs values are provided (e.g., duplicate coset points), the weight_adjustment computation (xs[i] - xs[j]).inverse() would attempt to invert zero, causing a crash or undefined behavior.\",\noutputs/01e_ASYNC_QUEUE_W5B14_1770296799.json:366:              \"description\": \"The recover_polynomialcoeff function (SG-023) involves multiple FFT operations, inverse FFT, coset FFT, and element-wise division over arrays of size FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_EXT_BLOB. An attacker could trigger recovery with carefully chosen inputs that maximize computational cost. Additionally, if missing_cell_indices results in a very large vanishing polynomial, the multiply_polynomialcoeff calls during construct_vanishing_polynomial could be expensive.\",\n\n[Showing results with pagination = limit: 30, offset: 0]"}]},"uuid":"9f3e9ce9-c064-4553-9dc7-f6faea1d82d4","timestamp":"2026-02-06T08:57:22.646Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"daee8868-e01f-49fd-81cb-5e413dcc4e5b"}
